<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>GoString - Tag - loommii</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/gostring/</link><description>我的个人技术博客</description><generator>Hugo 0.165.0 &amp; FixIt v0.4.5</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 16 Jan 2026 01:28:43 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://loommii.github.io/en/tags/gostring/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Go Daily Question 20250517</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_20250517/</link><pubDate>Sat, 17 May 2025 01:52:26 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_20250517/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;How many syntax issues are there in the following code, and what are they?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;func main() {&#10; var x string = nil&#10; if x == nil {&#10; x = &amp;#34;default&amp;#34;&#10; }&#10; fmt.Println(x)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;./prog.go:8:17: cannot use nil as string value in variable declaration&#10;./prog.go:9:10: invalid operation: x == nil (mismatched types string and untyped nil)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="var-x-string--nil-error"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;code&gt;var x string = nil&lt;/code&gt; Error&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#var-x-string--nil-error" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Go, the zero value for a &lt;code&gt;string&lt;/code&gt; type is an empty string &lt;code&gt;&amp;quot;&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;, not &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt; is typically used for types like pointers, slices, maps, channels, and interfaces. Therefore, assigning &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt; to a &lt;code&gt;string&lt;/code&gt; type variable &lt;code&gt;x&lt;/code&gt; will result in a compilation error.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 172</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_172/</link><pubDate>Sun, 26 Jan 2025 21:19:16 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_172/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;How many syntax issues are there in the following code, and what are they?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;func main() {&#10; var x string = nil&#10; if x == nil {&#10; x = &amp;#34;default&amp;#34;&#10; }&#10; fmt.Println(x)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;./prog.go:8:17: cannot use nil as string value in variable declaration&#10;./prog.go:9:10: invalid operation: x == nil (mismatched types string and untyped nil)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="var-x-string--nil-error"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;code&gt;var x string = nil&lt;/code&gt; Error&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#var-x-string--nil-error" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Go, the zero value for a &lt;code&gt;string&lt;/code&gt; type is an empty string &lt;code&gt;&amp;quot;&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;, not &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt; is typically used for types like pointers, slices, maps, channels, and interfaces. Therefore, assigning &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt; to a &lt;code&gt;string&lt;/code&gt; type variable &lt;code&gt;x&lt;/code&gt; will result in a compilation error.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 154</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_154/</link><pubDate>Tue, 07 Jan 2025 11:47:02 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_154/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {&#10; i := 65&#10; fmt.Println(string(i))&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A. A&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B. 65&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C. compilation error&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/lYfJ6qOVMJV" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Run online&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;string(i)&lt;/code&gt;: In Go, &lt;code&gt;string(int)&lt;/code&gt; interprets the integer as a Unicode code point and returns the corresponding character.&#10;65 is the ASCII code (and Unicode code point) for the character &amp;lsquo;A&amp;rsquo;, so &lt;code&gt;string(65)&lt;/code&gt; results in the string &amp;ldquo;A&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 148</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_148/</link><pubDate>Wed, 01 Jan 2025 13:08:46 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_148/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Which of the following options is correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;&#10;import &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;&#10;func main() {&#10; str := &amp;#34;hello&amp;#34;&#10; str[0] = &amp;#39;x&amp;#39;&#10; fmt.Println(str)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;A. hello&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;B. xello&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;C. compilation error&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ul&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;C&#10;&lt;a href="https://go.dev/play/p/WKuFSxIZZM8" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Run online&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://golang.google.cn/ref/spec#String_types" target="_blank" rel="external nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;Go Official Documentation - String Types&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Strings are immutable: once created, it is impossible to change the contents of a string.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;In the problem, &lt;code&gt;str&lt;/code&gt; is a string, and its content cannot be changed, so &lt;code&gt;str[0] = 'x'&lt;/code&gt; will cause an error.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 082</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_082/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:45:07 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_082/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;How many syntax issues are there in the following code, and what are they?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;package main&#10;import (&#10; &amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&#10;)&#10;func main() {&#10; var x string = nil&#10; if x == nil {&#10; x = &amp;#34;default&amp;#34;&#10; }&#10; fmt.Println(x)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;ol&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;var x string = nil&lt;/code&gt;&#10;A string cannot be &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;if x == nil {&lt;/code&gt;&#10;A string cannot be compared with &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ol&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 063</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_063/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:45 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_063/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() { &#10; i := 65&#10; fmt.Println(string(i))&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A. A&#10;B. 65&#10;C. compilation error&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;A&#10;In UTF-8 encoding, the decimal number 65 corresponds to the symbol &amp;lsquo;A&amp;rsquo;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 057</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_057/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:38 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_057/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Which of the following options is correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {&#10; str := &amp;#34;hello&amp;#34;&#10; str[0] = &amp;#39;x&amp;#39;&#10; fmt.Println(str)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A. hello&#10;B. xello&#10;C. compilation error&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Reference code and analysis: C.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Key takeaway: Strings in Go are read-only.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 051</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_051/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:30 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_051/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() { &#10; i := -5&#10; j := &amp;#43;5&#10; fmt.Printf(&amp;#34;%&amp;#43;d %&amp;#43;d&amp;#34;, i, j)&#10;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;A. -5 +5&#10;B. +5 +5&#10;C. 0 0&#10;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;A&#10;&lt;code&gt;%d&lt;/code&gt; indicates outputting a decimal number, and &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt; indicates outputting the sign of the number. It does not mean negation here.&#10;&lt;code&gt;%d&lt;/code&gt; output for positive numbers will not include the &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt; sign by default.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Go Daily Question 025</title><link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_025/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 19:49:56 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_025/</guid><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category><category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Regarding string concatenation, which of the following syntax is correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;A. str := &amp;#39;abc&amp;#39; &amp;#43; &amp;#39;123&amp;#39;&#10;B. str := &amp;#34;abc&amp;#34; &amp;#43; &amp;#34;123&amp;#34;&#10;C. str := &amp;#39;123&amp;#39; &amp;#43; &amp;#34;abc&amp;#34;&#10;D. fmt.Sprintf(&amp;#34;abc%d&amp;#34;, 123)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#10;&lt;details&gt;&#10; &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Answer: BD&#10;&lt;code&gt;' '&lt;/code&gt; can only be used for single characters, so A and C will not compile.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;For string concatenation, &lt;code&gt;buffer.WriteString()&lt;/code&gt; is recommended.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/details&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>